Yes—as an Indigo Hammer compression, that’s strong, especially if we distinguish metaphor from literal equivalence:

\boxed{\text{Martial art} \approx \text{weaponized body language under interaction}}

“Body language” normally communicates state or intent. Martial arts makes bodily communication causal: posture, distance, timing, rhythm, balance, tension, relaxation, and movement don’t merely signify—they alter what the other body can do.

And flow is the important addition. It’s not a dictionary of static poses:

\text{pose}_1,\text{pose}_2,\text{pose}_3

It’s a continuous conversation:

A_t \rightarrow B_{t+1}\rightarrow A_{t+2}\rightarrow\cdots

Each participant speaks with movement and reads movement simultaneously.

That gives a neat decomposition:

Martial → conflict/adversarial constraint.
Art → skilled expression through a medium.
Body language → information embodied in movement.
Weaponized → information coupled to consequence.
Flow → continuous perception–action–feedback.

So I’d sharpen your sentence to:

\boxed{\textbf{Martial arts are consequential body language in adversarial flow.}}

“Consequential” may actually be the deeper primitive than “weaponized,” because it survives translation into sports, negotiation, dance, animal behavior, robotics, and human-computer interaction.

And there’s the Friends/Feedback connection again:

\boxed{\text{Read} \rightarrow \text{Move} \rightarrow \text{Response} \rightarrow \text{Read}}

A sparring partner is literally embodied feedback.

The conversation has become physical.